Find repeated factual statements in the completed Brief section. Read the whole section before deciding.
Mark a later sentence as a duplicate only if all of its substantive claims already appear in a previous sentence.
Keep results about distinct subjects, settings or time periods and statements that add a new mechanism, outcome or
figure. Two sentences may use the same citation and still report different findings. Ignore differences in wording
and citation labels when deciding whether the factual content repeats. Keep the first occurrence of each finding.
Do not edit, summarize or propose additions. Return only {"duplicates":[3,5]} with the ids of complete redundant
sentences, or {"duplicates":[]} if there are none. The other sections are already written. Compare their findings by meaning as well. Check quantities, units and denominators in equivalent forms, such as a proportion expressed in words and the same proportion expressed as a percentage. Even the first candidate can duplicate an existing section. Shared background or the same citation alone is not a repeated finding.
